# LeakSanitizer suppressions for cache-turbo's ASan legs.
#
# WHAT IS SUPPRESSED, AND WHY IT IS NOT A LEAK
#
# nginx allocates its configuration out of the cycle pool (cf->pool) and frees
# it by destroying that pool, not by freeing individual blocks. On the
# `nginx -t` path the process exits after the config check without tearing the
# cycle pool down, so at exit LeakSanitizer sees every config-time allocation
# still reachable from a pool that was never destroyed.
#
# Every one of those reports is an INDIRECT leak -- the block is reachable from
# the pool block, which is itself unfreed. None of them is a block whose last
# pointer was dropped, which is what a real leak looks like.
#
# Measured on nginx 1.31.3, ASan build, 2026-08-25:
#
#   config WITHOUT any cache_turbo_backend preset:
#     12 allocations / 120968 bytes, ZERO cache-turbo frames -- pure core
#     (ngx_http_variables_add_core_vars, ngx_http_proxy_init_headers, ...)
#
#   config WITH `cache_turbo_backend wordpress`:
#     13 allocations / 220040 bytes -- the same 12, plus one 99072-byte node
#     array from ngx_http_cache_turbo_cookie_ac_prepare()
#
# That extra block is ngx_palloc(cf->pool, ...) in cookie_ac_prepare(), holding
# the Aho-Corasick node table for the auto-Vary cookie matcher. It is owned by
# cf->pool for the life of the configuration and is correctly never freed by
# hand; adding a cleanup handler to release it would mean hand-managing memory
# the pool already owns, and would still leave the 12 core reports standing.
#
# WHY THE KEY IS ngx_init_cycle
#
# ngx_init_cycle appears in all 13 stacks and in no runtime allocation -- it is
# the config-parse entry point, called once from main() before any request is
# served. Keying on it suppresses exactly the config-time pool population and
# nothing else.
#
# This is deliberately NOT keyed on ngx_palloc / ngx_pcalloc / ngx_alloc. Those
# are the leaf allocators for the whole server, request pools included, so
# suppressing them would blind the ASan legs to every real leak the module
# could introduce on the hot path. The point of this file is to keep
# detect_leaks=1 meaningful, not to switch leak detection off -- the blunt
# ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 it replaces did the latter.
#
# A leak in module code that is NOT reachable from the config pool -- anything
# allocated per-request, per-connection, or from the shared zone -- still has a
# stack without ngx_init_cycle in it and still fails the build.
#
# NOT SOLVED HERE: the short-lived `nginx -s reload` / `nginx -t` signaller
# leaks its own 1024-byte init_cycle pool (src/core/nginx.c:254), on a stack
# whose only frames are ngx_memalign <- ngx_create_pool <- main. No entry was
# added for it, because all three frames were MEASURED too broad to key on:
# LSan matches a suppression against ANY frame on the allocation stack, so
# `leak:ngx_create_pool` also hides a pool the module itself leaks, and
# `leak:main` hides everything in a single-process build. That one is handled
# by scoping detect_leaks=0 to the throwaway process instead -- see
# _throwaway_nginx_env() in ci/tools/test_runtime_base.py. Keep it there: doing
# it here would blind the server's own leak check, which is what this file
# exists to preserve.

leak:ngx_init_cycle
